data.content:
All four scenarios work. Here's what the program demonstrates:

**`GetTypeInfo<T>()`** — returns a strongly-typed `JsonTypeInfo<T>` directly (no casting needed). We use it to serialize a `Person` and get back `{"name":"Alice","age":42}`.

**`TryGetTypeInfo<T>(out JsonTypeInfo<T>?)`** — probes for metadata without throwing:
- With a resolver configured → returns `true` + usable `JsonTypeInfo<Person>` for deserialization.
- With `TypeInfoResolver = null` → returns `false`, no exception.

Both APIs are new in .NET 11 on `JsonSerializerOptions`. The final `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata;

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web)
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()
};

// 1. GetTypeInfo<T>() — strongly-typed metadata
JsonTypeInfo<Person> personInfo = options.GetTypeInfo<Person>();
string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new Person("Alice", 42), personInfo);

// 2. TryGetTypeInfo<T>() — safe probe (returns false instead of throwing)
if (options.TryGetTypeInfo<Person>(out var info))
    JsonSerializer.Deserialize(json, info!);

// No resolver → returns false, no exception
var empty = new JsonSerializerOptions { TypeInfoResolver = null };
empty.TryGetTypeInfo<Person>(out _); // false

public record Person(string Name, int Age);
```
